### 1\. Core Mathematical Formulas

Before diving into the algorithm, it is crucial to understand the foundational equations that drive these models.

**The Gaussian Probability Density Function (Continuous Model):**
$$P(x)=\frac{1}{\sigma\sqrt{2\pi}}e^{-\frac{(x-\mu)^{2}}{2\sigma^{2}}}$$

  * **$x$**: The input data point.
  * [cite_start]**$\mu$**: The mean (center) of the distribution[cite: 48].
  * **$\sigma$**: The standard deviation (spread) of the distribution.
  * [cite_start]**$\Sigma$**: The covariance matrix (for multivariate data)[cite: 49].

**Mixture Distribution Formula:**
$$P(x)=\sum_{i=1}^{k}P(C=i)P(x|C=i)$$

  * [cite_start]**$k$**: The total number of components in the mixture[cite: 42].
  * [cite_start]**$C$**: The random variable denoting a specific component[cite: 45].
  * [cite_start]**$P(C=i)$**: The weight ($w_i$) or prior probability of component $i$[cite: 48].
  * [cite_start]**$P(x|C=i)$**: The probability of data point $x$ given it belongs to component $i$[cite: 61].

**Log-Likelihood Objective Function:**
$$L=\sum_{j=1}^{N}\log\left(\frac{1}{\sigma\sqrt{2\pi}}e^{-\frac{(x_{j}-\mu)^{2}}{2\sigma^{2}}}\right)$$

  * [cite_start]**$L$**: The log-likelihood, which the algorithm seeks to maximize to find the best-fitting parameters[cite: 5, 69].
  * [cite_start]**$N$**: The total number of data points[cite: 5].

