### 2\. Summary: The EM Algorithm for GMMs

[cite_start]A Gaussian Mixture Model assumes that the data is generated by a combination of $k$ different Gaussian distributions, each with its own weight, mean, and covariance[cite: 46, 47, 48, 49]. [cite_start]If we knew which component generated which data point, calculating the parameters would be simple[cite: 56]. Because we do not, we use the EM algorithm.

[cite_start]The EM algorithm starts by initializing parameters arbitrarily[cite: 59, 64]. [cite_start]It then alternates between two steps until the log-likelihood converges and stops increasing[cite: 69, 88].

| Phase | Purpose | Key Mathematical Operations |
| :--- | :--- | :--- |
| **E-Step (Expectation)** | [cite_start]Calculates the probability that each data point belongs to each component[cite: 60]. | [cite_start]$$p_{ij}=\alpha P(x_{j}|C=i)P(C=i)$$ Computes $p_{ij}$, the "soft assignment" of datum $x_j$ to component $i$ using Bayes' rule[cite: 60, 61]. |
| **M-Step (Maximization)** | [cite_start]Updates the parameters of the Gaussians based on the probabilities calculated in the E-step[cite: 66]. | **1. Effective Count:** $n_{i}=\sum_{j}p_{ij}$ <br> **2. New Mean:** $$\mu_{i}\leftarrow\frac{\sum_{j}p_{ij}x_{j}}{n_{i}}$$<br> **3. New Covariance:**$$\Sigma_{i}\leftarrow\frac{\sum_{j}p_{ij}(x_{j}-\mu_{i})(x_{j}-\mu_{i})^{\top}}{n_{i}}$$ <br> **4. [cite_start]New Weight:** $w_{i}\leftarrow\frac{n_{i}}{N}$ [cite: 67, 68] |

